  "articleBody": "Many homeowners wonder if greenboard moisture-resistant drywall is sufficient for their tub or shower renovation. Understanding the difference between moisture-resistant gypsum and cement backer board protects your home from costly water damage.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice\nThe location of the wall determines the material you must install:\n- Direct Water Zones (Tub Surrounds & Shower Enclosures): Cement backer board, fiber-cement, or glass-mat panels are required. Standard drywall and greenboard are not rated for prolonged direct water exposure and will soften, degrade, and drop tile if moisture gets behind the grout.\n- General Bathroom Walls & Ceilings: Moisture-resistant gypsum board (often called greenboard) is ideal for walls outside the immediate shower stall. It handles humid ambient air and occasional splashes near the vanity, but it cannot stand up to continuous direct spray.\n- The Waterproofing Factor: Cement board itself is water-durable, meaning it will not disintegrate when wet, but it remains porous. A liquid or sheet waterproof membrane must be applied over cement board to stop moisture from migrating into the wall studs behind it.\n\nChoosing the proper wall assembly prevents tile failure and structural rot. HH Remodeling LLC provides expert bathroom framing, drywall installation, and tile setting across Denver, Centennial, Aurora, and Lakewood. Call Abraham Becerra at 303-505-0866 to plan your bathroom renovation properly.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
